#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:9
msgid "How ROS works"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:12
msgid ""
"The ROS framework is the “plumbing” which allows for communication between "
"parts of a robot, and within parts of a robot. This article provides an "
"overview of how the main components work together to enable this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:21
msgid "Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:24
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:26
msgid ""
"ROS systems use a network of modular software elements, called nodes, which "
"process data concurrently and can communicate with each other through "
"defined interfaces. The ROS graph refers to the network of nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:30
msgid "Elements of a ROS system"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:32
msgid ""
"The ROS framework consists of multiple elements which allow you to build a "
"complete robotics system that can receive data from sensors and transform "
"that data into robotic actions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:34
msgid ""
"A full robotic system consists of many nodes working together. In ROS, a "
"single executable (C++ program, Python program, etc.) can contain one or "
"more nodes. Each node in ROS should be responsible for a single, modular "
"purpose, such as controlling a motor or publishing sensor data. ROS nodes "
"can process data concurrently and can communicate with each other. "
"See :doc:`About-Nodes`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:40
msgid ""
"Communication between nodes is managed using the middleware interface. See "
"the Discovery section of :doc:`About-Nodes`, and :doc:`client-libraries/"
"About-Internal-Interfaces/About-Internal-Interfaces`. Each node can send "
"data to and receive data from other nodes. See :doc:`Interfaces-Topics-"
"Services-Actions`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:45
msgid ""
"Parameters are used to configure nodes. Parameters can be set at startup and "
"can be modified during runtime. See :doc:`About-Parameters`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:49
msgid ""
"Client libraries are the APIs that allow you to implement your ROS code. "
"Client libraries implement code generators which allow you to interact with "
"ROS interface files. These interface files then allow nodes written with "
"different client libraries to exchange messages. See :doc:`About-Client-"
"Libraries`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:55
msgid "The ROS graph"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:57
msgid ""
"The *ROS graph* describes the nodes in a system and their relationship to "
"each other."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:59
msgid ""
"For example, in this simplified diagram, multiple nodes communicate with "
"each other through different interfaces including a topic, a service, and an "
"action. Real systems often combine several of these patterns inside the same "
"nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:70
msgid ""
"The ROS graph can be visualized in multiple ways. For example, the "
"visualized ROS graph using rqt in :doc:`interfaces/topics/Understanding-ROS2-"
"Topics/Understanding-ROS2-Topics` shows two nodes, ``turtlesim`` and "
"``teleop_turtle``. The ``/teleop_turtle`` node publishes data to the ``/"
"turtle1/cmd_vel`` topic, and the ``/turtlesim`` node is subscribed to that "
"topic to receive the data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:77
msgid "ROS CLI and client libraries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:79
msgid "You can build with ROS using the command line and the ROS API."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:81
msgid "ROS CLI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:82
msgid ""
"The ROS command line tools allow you to interact with your ROS system using "
"the command line interface (CLI). You can use the CLI for a variety of tasks "
"including launching or investigating nodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:85
msgid "ROS API"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:86
msgid ""
"The ROS API allows you to build and run complex systems using client "
"libraries. For a more in-depth description of the ROS API, see :doc:`About-"
"Client-Libraries`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/How-ROS-Works.rst:90
msgid ""
"If you are new to ROS, working with the CLI will help you understand ROS "
"elements and how to interact with them. ROS command line tools allow you to "
"explore a live graph before writing your own nodes, and client libraries let "
"you implement the same concepts in code. See :doc:`../First-Steps`."
msgstr ""

